/-
# Varieties of quasigroups isotopic to groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 9.4)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 9.4 (A. A. Gvaramiya), marked solved in the 21st issue: if `𝔐` is a
variety (quasivariety, pseudovariety) of groups, then the class `I𝔐` of all quasigroups isotopic to
groups in `𝔐` is also a variety (quasivariety, pseudovariety), and `I𝔐` is finitely based iff `𝔐`
is. Is it true that if `𝔐` is generated by a single finite group then `I𝔐` is generated by a single
finite quasigroup?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Basic.Finite.Defs
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FreeGroup.Basic
public import WantedExt.Algebra.FiniteAlgebraFiniteBasisKourovka2Problem41Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.QuasigroupIsotopeVarietyKourovka9Problem4Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 9.4 (A. A. Gvaramiya). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00700539 (`KOU-9.4`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The variety case is formalized; the quasivariety and pseudovariety readings are not. Quasigroups are
the ordinary equational quasigroups (operations `*`, `\`, `/` with the four cancellation laws), the
interpretation of the resolving note, which makes no claim for Gvaramiya's three-sorted reversible
automata. The variety generated by a finite group `G₀` is the class of groups satisfying every law
of `G₀`; a quasigroup is isotopic to a group `H` if there are bijections `α β γ` with `γ(x * y) = α
x · β y`. By Birkhoff's theorem, `I𝔐` (a variety, by the result quoted in the problem) is generated
by a finite quasigroup `Q` iff its members are exactly the quasigroups satisfying every identity of
`Q`. Quasigroups are non-empty: the empty algebra satisfies every identity and is isotopic to no
group. Terms over `x₀, x₁, …` and their values are `QTerm` and `QTerm.eval` of the Kourovka 2.41
entry, evaluated through the `QOps` triple `(*, \, /)`. The resolution is an AI-generated solution
(Kinyon-Urban, GPT-6 Astra via Codex) that the Notebook records as verified by K. Muliarchyk.
-/

open MathlibExt.Algebra.FiniteAlgebraFiniteBasisKourovka2Problem41Wanted (QTerm QOps)

/-- A quasigroup in the equational sense: binary operations `*`, `\` and `/` on `Q` with
`x \ (x * y) = y`, `x * (x \ y) = y`, `(x * y) / y = x` and `(x / y) * y = x`. -/
structure QuasigroupOps (Q : Type*) where
  /-- Multiplication `x * y`. -/
  mul : Q → Q → Q
  /-- Left division `x \ y`. -/
  ldiv : Q → Q → Q
  /-- Right division `x / y`. -/
  rdiv : Q → Q → Q
  ldiv_mul : ∀ x y, ldiv x (mul x y) = y
  mul_ldiv : ∀ x y, mul x (ldiv x y) = y
  mul_rdiv : ∀ x y, rdiv (mul x y) y = x
  rdiv_mul : ∀ x y, mul (rdiv x y) y = x

/-- The operations `(*, \, /)` of the quasigroup `(Q, S)` as a `QOps` triple. -/
def QuasigroupOps.toQOps {Q : Type} (S : QuasigroupOps Q) : QOps Q :=
  (S.mul, S.ldiv, S.rdiv)

/-- The quasigroup `(Q, S)` satisfies the identity `s = t`. -/
def SatisfiesIdentity {Q : Type} (S : QuasigroupOps Q) (s t : QTerm) : Prop :=
  ∀ v : ℕ → Q, s.eval S.toQOps v = t.eval S.toQOps v

/-- The group `H` lies in the variety generated by the group `G₀`: every law of `G₀` (a word
`w ∈ FreeGroup ℕ` with `f w = 1` for every homomorphism `f` to `G₀`) holds in `H`. -/
def InGroupVariety (G₀ H : Type*) [Group G₀] [Group H] : Prop :=
  ∀ w : FreeGroup ℕ, (∀ f : FreeGroup ℕ →* G₀, f w = 1) → ∀ f : FreeGroup ℕ →* H, f w = 1

/-- The quasigroup `(Q, S)` is isotopic to the group `H`: there are bijections `α β γ : Q ≃ H` with
`γ (x * y) = α x * β y` for all `x, y`. -/
def IsIsotopicToGroup {Q : Type*} (S : QuasigroupOps Q) (H : Type*) [Group H] : Prop :=
  ∃ α β γ : Q ≃ H, ∀ x y, γ (S.mul x y) = α x * β y

/-- Kourovka 9.4 (A. A. Gvaramiya): if the variety `𝔐` of groups is generated by a single finite
group `G₀`, then the variety `I𝔐` of quasigroups isotopic to groups in `𝔐` is generated by a single
finite quasigroup `(Q, S)`: a (non-empty) quasigroup lies in `I𝔐` if and only if it satisfies every
identity of `(Q, S)`. -/
def isotopeVarietyGeneratedByFiniteQuasigroup : Prop :=
  ∀ (G₀ : Type) [Group G₀] [Finite G₀], ∃ (Q : Type) (_ : Finite Q) (_ : Nonempty Q)
    (S : QuasigroupOps Q), ∀ (R : Type) [Nonempty R] (T : QuasigroupOps R),
      (∃ (H : Type) (_ : Group H), InGroupVariety G₀ H ∧ IsIsotopicToGroup T H) ↔
        ∀ s t : QTerm, SatisfiesIdentity S s t → SatisfiesIdentity T s t

/--
Resolved false: Kinyon and Urban (Section 3.3) show that no finite quasigroup generates the isotope
variety `I𝔐` of a non-trivial finite group: for `C_p` inside the group, the quasigroup `a * b = a +
T(b)` on `⊕_{i ∈ ℤ} 𝔽_p` (`T` the shift) is isotopic to a group in `𝔐`, infinite and one-generated,
whereas the variety generated by a finite quasigroup is locally finite; the Kourovka Notebook (21st
issue) marks 9.4 solved: "No, it is not true" (AI solutions verified by K. Muliarchyk). Source: E.
I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st
issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 9.4 (A. A. Gvaramiya); resolution: M. Kinyon and J. Urban,
Results on problems from the Kourovka Notebook, preprint, version 4 (19 September 2026), Section
3.3, Question 9.4 ("No finite quasigroup generates the ordinary isotope variety of a nontrivial
finite group"; scope: the ordinary one-sorted variety of quasigroups in the signature (*, \, /)):
for a subgroup C_p of the finite group, the quasigroup a * b = a + T(b) on the bilateral sum of
copies of F_p (T the shift) lies in I𝔐 and is infinite and one-generated, while the variety
generated by a finite algebra is locally finite; the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 9.4
solved: "No, it is not true" (AI solutions verified by K. Muliarchyk),
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/kourovka-mathematics.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted isotopeVarietyGeneratedByFiniteQuasigroup_refuted :
    ¬ isotopeVarietyGeneratedByFiniteQuasigroup

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.QuasigroupIsotopeVarietyKourovka9Problem4Wanted
